## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design cross-protocol schema alignment and implement practical versioning strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-protocol schema mapping from canonical model
- Unified internal service layer with protocol adapters
- Consistent versioning across all protocols
- Contract validation and routing patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Canonical model adapter
function toInternalBook(restInput) {
  return {
    id: restInput.id || generateId(),
    title: restInput.title,
    author: restInput.author,
    stock: restInput.stock || 0
  };
}

// Protocol-agnostic service layer
const bookService = {
  async getBook(id) {
    return await database.books.findById(id);
  },
  async createBook(bookData) {
    const internalBook = toInternalBook(bookData);
    return await database.books.create(internalBook);
  }
};

// Protocol adapters
const restAdapter = {
  getBook: async (req, res) => {
    const book = await bookService.getBook(req.params.id);
    res.json(book);
  },
  createBook: async (req, res) => {
    const book = await bookService.createBook(req.body);
    res.status(201).json(book);
  }
};

// GraphQL schema
type Book {
  id: ID!
  title: String!
  author: String!
  stock: Int!
}

type Query {
  getBook(id: ID!): Book
}

type Mutation {
  addBook(title: String!, author: String!, stock: Int): Book
}

// gRPC service definition
service BookService {
  rpc GetBook(GetBookRequest) returns (Book);
  rpc CreateBook(CreateBookRequest) returns (Book);
}

// Versioning patterns
// REST: /api/v1/books
// GraphQL: Use @deprecated directive for v2 changes
// gRPC: package v1.bookservice;
```

## Implementation Strategy
1. Define canonical domain model first
2. Create protocol-specific schemas that map to the canonical model
3. Implement adapter layer for request/response transformation
4. Build unified service layer with business logic
5. Apply consistent versioning strategy across all protocols

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-service orchestration, eventual consistency, and fault handling in microservices that use event sourcing and CQRS. It also probes patterns like outbox, idempotency, and compensating actions.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across multiple bounded contexts
- Event contracts and versioning for backward compatibility
- Outbox pattern for at-least-once delivery and durability
- Idempotent handlers keyed by correlationId
- Compensation actions to maintain consistency

## Code Example
```typescript
type Event = { type: string; payload: any; correlationId: string };
type Command = { type: string; payload: any; correlationId: string };

function handleEvent(e: Event) {
  const { type, payload, correlationId } = e;
  switch (type) {
    case 'CartCheckedOut':
      enqueue({ type: 'InventoryReserve', payload, correlationId });
      break;
    case 'InventoryReserved':
      enqueue({ type: 'PaymentCharge', payload, correlationId });
      break;
    case 'PaymentApproved':
      enqueue({ type: 'FinalizeOrder', payload, correlationId });
      break;
    case 'PaymentDeclined':
    case 'InventoryReserveFailed':
      enqueue({ type: 'CancelOrder', payload, correlationId });
      break;
  }
  // simple dedupe check
  if (alreadyProcessed(correlationId, type)) return;
  markProcessed(correlationId, type);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency guarantees across retries and outages?
- What metrics and tracing would you add to observe saga progress and failures?

Why This Is Asked
- Tests cache-aside pattern understanding and cross-tenant isolation under real workloads
- Probes invalidation strategies, TTL optimization, and stampede defenses
- Evaluates practical use of Redis features (keyspace notifications, pub/sub) and distributed locking

Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern with per-tenant namespace isolation
- TTL optimization for hot versus cold data
- Negative caching for cache misses
- Redis keyspace notifications and PUB/SUB for targeted invalidation
- Distributed locking (SETNX/Redlock) to prevent stampede
- Fallback to Memcached with consistency considerations

Code Example
```javascript
// getProduct with cache-aside pattern
async function getProduct(tenantId, productId) {
  const cacheKey = `tenant:${tenantId}:product:${productId}`;

  // Check cache first
  let cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (cached) {
    return JSON.parse(cached);
  }

  // Cache miss - fetch from database
  const product = await db.query(
    'SELECT * FROM products WHERE tenant_id = ? AND id = ?',
    [tenantId, productId]
  );

  if (product) {
    const ttl = product.isHot ? 300 : 60; // 5min hot, 1min cold
    await redis.setex(cacheKey, ttl, JSON.stringify(product));
  } else {
    // Negative caching - cache misses too
    await redis.setex(cacheKey, 30, '');
  }

  return product;
}

// updateProduct with invalidation and stampede protection
async function updateProduct(tenantId, productId, updates) {
  const lockKey = `lock:tenant:${tenantId}:product:${productId}`;
  const cacheKey = `tenant:${tenantId}:product:${productId}`;

  // Acquire distributed lock
  const lock = await redis.set(lockKey, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 10);
  if (!lock) {
    throw new Error('Concurrent update in progress');
  }

  try {
    // Perform database update
    await db.query(
      'UPDATE products SET ? WHERE tenant_id = ? AND id = ?',
      [updates, tenantId, productId]
    );

    // Invalidate cache - delete immediately
    await redis.del(cacheKey);

    // Publish invalidation event for this tenant
    await redis.publish(
      `invalidation:tenant:${tenantId}`,
      JSON.stringify({ productId, action: 'updated' })
    );

  } finally {
    await redis.del(lockKey);
  }
}

// Subscribe to invalidation events
async function subscribeToInvalidations(tenantId) {
  const subscriber = redis.duplicate();
  await subscriber.subscribe(`invalidation:tenant:${tenantId}`);

  subscriber.on('message', async (channel, message) => {
    if (channel === `invalidation:tenant:${tenantId}`) {
      const { productId } = JSON.parse(message);
      console.log(`Invalidated: tenant=${tenantId}, product=${productId}`);
    }
  });
}
```

Key Takeaways
- Always namespace keys by tenant to ensure complete isolation
- Use negative caching to prevent repeated database queries for missing items
- Implement distributed locks on writes to avoid thundering herd on cache miss
- Publish invalidation events for multi-instance deployments to synchronize cache state
- Choose TTLs based on data volatility - hot products get longer TTLs

## Why This Is Asked

Contract-driven, multi-protocol API surfaces with per-tenant policies are a common real-world challenge. This question probes how to orchestrate versioning, enforcement, and migration across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC while preserving contract compatibility.

## Key Concepts

- Central, versioned Contract Registry per tenant
- Runtime field visibility enforced in REST, GraphQL, and gRPC adapters
- Canary migrations and cross-protocol compatibility checks
- Contract drift detection and end-to-end validation

## Code Example

```javascript
type Policy = { fieldsVisible: string[] };
function applyPolicy(record, policy: Policy) {
  const out = {};
  policy.fieldsVisible.forEach((f) => {
    if (f in record) out[f] = record[f];
  });
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cross-protocol compatibility during policy changes?
- What metrics and dashboards would you use to detect contract drift and rollout health?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world need to ensure token binding prevents replay across tenants and regions.

## Key Concepts
- DPoP proof binding
- per-tenant JWKS rotation
- replay protection
- token audience and scope mapping

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node-ish sketch: validate DPoP and issue bound JWT
async function issueBoundJwt(accessToken, dpopHeader, tenant){
  const jwk = await fetchTenantJwks(tenant);
  const valid = await verifyDPoP(dpopHeader, jwk, accessToken);
  if(!valid) throw new Error('DPoP invalid');
  const payload = mapToInternalClaims(parseToken(accessToken), tenant);
  return signJwt(payload, {aud: tenant.aud, iss: 'gateway'});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle cross-tenant revocation in a global cache?
- What are the failure modes for DPoP binding in roaming clients?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of token binding and multi-idp flows in production.

## Key Concepts
- Token binding with cnf
- per-tenant key rotation
- KMS/JWK management
- auditing and revocation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Issuer (simplified)
const tenantKwk = getTenantKey(tenantId); // {kid, jwk}
const token = jwt.sign({sub, iss: 'gateway', aud: tenantAud}, tenantPrivateKey, { algorithm:'ES256', header: { kid: tenantKwk.kid }, nonce: crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex'), cnf: { jwk: tenantKwk.jwk } });

// Verifier
const decoded = jwt.verify(token, tenantPublicKey, { algorithms:['ES256'] });
if (!compareJwk(decoded.cnf.jwk, getTenantBoundJwk(tenantId))) throw new Error('binding mismatch');
```

## Follow-up Questions
